- in_text_citation: [(Uscumlic, and Blankertz, 2016-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/992d43aff1aa73adc10255dcbc63e3c994300088)
- venue: Journal of Neural Engineering
- citation count: 27
[method]
The paper used a simultaneous EEG and eye-tracking active visual search paradigm in which participants silently counted target Landolt rings among serially presented non-targets under three stimulus appearance protocols: pop-up (PU), smooth appearance (SA), and motion appearance (MA). This is related to the proposed research plan because it provides a target-monitoring EEG dataset with serial visual stimuli and no motor responses during the critical viewing periods, and it uses LDA-based temporal decoding of fixation-related EEG, which is conceptually aligned with the plan's use of linear discriminant analysis to examine neural correlates of expected and unexpected visual events. However, the paper does not directly implement the proposed micro, meso, and macro temporal-context manipulations, task-related versus task-unrelated spatial planes, or inverted encoding modeling. Fixation-related EEG epochs were extracted around fixation onset and classified as target versus non-target using shrinkage LDA and hierarchical discriminant component analysis (HDCA). Intra-protocol HDCA used non-overlapping 100-ms windows from 100 to 800 ms after fixation onset in a ten-fold cross-validation setup. Inter-protocol transfer trained an HDCA classifier on the most discriminant PU interval, 200 to 600 ms, corresponding to four 100-ms windows, and applied it in a sliding manner to SA and MA data with 50-ms shifts starting at 200 ms. The final trial decision used the maximum posterior probability across the seven sliding positions. A sliding-window intra-protocol control was also performed, and frontal channels were excluded in a separate analysis to assess the influence of eye-movement-related EEG activity. Spatiotemporal discriminability was also characterized using a separability measure and scalp maps.
[results]
Behaviorally, participants correctly reported the number of targets in 89.55% of PU sequences, 88.18% of SA sequences, and 46.89% of MA sequences. Mean response errors were -0.61 +/- 1.08 for PU, -0.077 +/- 2.24 for SA, and -1.25 +/- 1.06 for MA. Ocularly, median gaze response times were 240 ms for PU, 590 ms for SA, and 1.32 s for MA, with no target versus non-target difference in gaze response in any condition. Target versus non-target differences in first fixation duration were significant for 4 of 16 subjects in PU, 5 of 16 in SA, and 9 of 16 in MA. Target versus non-target differences in dwell time were significant for 5 of 16 subjects in PU, 6 of 16 in SA, and 15 of 16 in MA. For EEG, single-trial target versus non-target classification was above chance in all three presentation protocols, with the highest performance in PU and similar performance in SA and MA. Inter-protocol transfer from PU improved classification in SA and MA relative to treating each condition individually. The intermediate sliding-window classification scores for the transferred classifier peaked at 100 to 150 ms for SA and MA, indicating delayed discriminant activity relative to PU. Sliding-window intra-protocol decoding improved performance only slightly, supporting the interpretation that the inter-protocol gain mainly came from transfer of knowledge from PU rather than from temporal sliding alone. Excluding very frontal EEG channels did not produce a significant change in classification performance at the 1% significance level. Specific AUC values were not reported in the extracted text beyond the statement that performance was above the 0.5 chance level.
[context]
The study included 16 healthy participants, 13 male and 3 female, aged 19 to 48 years. EEG was recorded with 63 active electrodes at 1000 Hz and re-referenced to the linked mastoid. Eye movements were recorded with a remote eye tracker at 250 Hz. The display was 1680 by 1050 pixels, 47.2 by 29.6 cm, corresponding to approximately 42 by 27 degrees of visual angle, with a viewing distance of approximately 61 cm and a chin rest. The region of interest for gaze analysis was a 100-pixel circle centered on each stimulus, corresponding to approximately 2.5 degrees of visual angle. Fixation detection used a peak velocity threshold of 40 deg/s, reported in the extracted text as 40 s, and a minimum fixation duration of 50 ms. Stimuli were eight broken Landolt ring orientations, with a target probability of 25% and an individual non-target probability of approximately 10.7%. In the PU condition, stimuli appeared with random inter-stimulus intervals of 1 to 1.5 s; SA and MA included fading-in, enlarging, and motion effects. Classification performance was evaluated using the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, with chance estimated by label permutation in a ten-fold cross-validation setup. Statistical comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank or rank-sum tests with Bonferroni correction where appropriate. The dataset involves active viewing with natural eye movements, and the EEG analysis is fixation-locked target versus non-target decoding rather than spatial location decoding or direct testing of micro, meso, and macro temporal context.
[end related work 1]


