[method]
The paper used a spatial cueing paradigm to direct transient covert attention to one of two peripheral locations and then measured whether the attended stimulus appeared different from the unattended one. Observers fixated, a brief peripheral cue indicated which of two locations would be attended, and after a delay Gabor patches were presented at the cued and uncued locations. Observers made a binary left/right two-alternative forced-choice judgment about which patch appeared brighter or higher in contrast. This relates to the proposed method because both use a task-defined left/right spatial reference to index attention and test whether attention changes perceptual judgments, although the paper did not implement the proposed orthogonal task-unrelated above/below plane or temporal-history sorting.
[results]
Cued patches were judged as brighter or higher contrast more often than uncued patches, with choices significantly above the 50% chance baseline. The paper reported this as a significant cueing effect, interpreted as an attention-induced change in appearance rather than a response bias, and the cue was treated as effective because it reliably directed attention to the cued location.
[context]
The experiment used a small group of human observers, Gabor patches at two peripheral locations around fixation, a brief spatial cue with a delay before stimulus presentation, and a two-alternative forced-choice appearance judgment. The design controlled for cue validity and response bias, and the stimuli were presented on a calibrated display under controlled luminance conditions.
[end related work 1]



