[method]
The paper used a speeded two-alternative forced-choice perceptual decision task to collect behavioral outcome measures analogous to the proposed response time and accuracy measures. Participants judged whether a circular field of oriented bars had a mean rotation of 45 degrees or 135 degrees while the bar field flickered at 15 Hz over an 8 Hz contrast-noise background. Each trial included 750 ms of fixation, a 750 ms noise-only interval, a response interval sampled uniformly from 1000 to 2000 ms, and a 250 ms post-response fixation period. Task difficulty was manipulated using three bar-rotation variance levels and three contrast-noise levels, with 90 trials per condition and 540 trials per participant. Response time and accuracy were recorded, no-answer trials were scored as incorrect, and auditory feedback was provided after the response interval. EEG was recorded with a 128-channel system, filtered, cleaned using independent component analysis, and steady-state visual evoked potentials were quantified using phase locking index values for signal and noise frequencies and harmonics. Phase locking index variables were reduced with principal component analysis and used to constrain a hierarchical Bayesian diffusion model of response time and accuracy. The diffusion model estimated drift rate, diffusion coefficient, and non-decision time, while boundary separation was fixed to one evidence unit and bias was fixed to 0.5. This relates to the proposed method because it measures response time and accuracy in a speeded binary visual judgment and links behavior to neural attentional measures, but it does not measure recall precision or sort trials by task-related versus task-unrelated reference planes.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports only model convergence: for all models and parameters, Monte Carlo chain convergence was satisfactory with R <= 1.01, below the conventional non-convergence threshold of 1.10. No quantitative response time, accuracy, recall precision, or reference-plane comparisons are reported in the provided text, and no statistical significance values for behavioral outcomes are available.
[context]
The experiment was conducted in a dark room with a monitor, and the bar field subtended 9.5 degrees of visual angle. Bar-rotation variance levels were uniform distributions centered on the mean angle with half-widths of 30, 35, and 40 degrees. Contrast-noise levels were 30%, 45%, and 60%, implemented by adding uniform luminance noise around a 50% baseline. Participants completed 36 training trials, optional additional training, and six blocks of 90 trials. EEG was sampled at 1024 Hz, high-pass filtered at 1 Hz, low-pass filtered at 50 Hz, and notch filtered around 60 Hz; artifacts were removed using independent component analysis and amplitude-based trial rejection. Phase locking index was computed over approximately 540 trials for each electrode and frequency, using prefrontal, frontal, central, parietal, and occipital electrode groups. The participant count is not specified in the provided excerpt.
