- in_text_citation: [(Yashiro et al., 2023-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5b421d75655192e7ecc68ba4812bd186411e8aad)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 3
[method]
The paper used an EEG-based decoding pipeline for visual orientation representations that is methodologically related to the proposed plan’s use of EEG, visual stimuli, feature decoding over time, and cluster-corrected statistics. In the paper’s EEG experiments, participants viewed six Gabor patterns for 500 ms and made button judgments about either a single uniform orientation or the average orientation of a multi-orientation set. EEG epochs were cleaned with independent component analysis and then analyzed using linear classification and inverted encoding models. The inverted encoding model estimated six channel responses to 180 orientations ranging from -89 degrees to 90 degrees at each time point. The decoded orientation at each time point was selected as the orientation whose channel response showed the strongest correlation with the cross-decoded response, constrained to orientations with the same sign as the stimulus set average. These decoded orientation time courses were then correlated with participants’ perceived average orientations obtained from a separate continuous bar-adjustment experiment. Cluster-based permutation tests were used to identify significant time windows. This relates to the proposed research method because both approaches use EEG to track visual feature representations over time and use multivariate decoding plus cluster-based inference, but the paper’s method is not a re-analysis of a previously published target-monitoring dataset and it includes motor responses, so it is analogous rather than directly matching the proposed motor-confound-free data source.
[results]
The inverted encoding model showed a peak in channel response for the average orientation around 400-700 ms after stimulus onset. A sensitivity measure derived from the channel response profile was significantly elevated at time points identified by a cluster-based permutation test with cluster-defining threshold p < 0.05 and cluster threshold p < 0.05. Correlations between the decoded orientation and participants’ perceived average orientation were significant from 568-668 ms for stimulus sets containing an element matching the average orientation, and from 640-698 ms and 722-766 ms for stimulus sets without such an element. These results indicate that an ensemble representation matching individual perception was present by approximately 600-700 ms after stimulus onset. Perceived average orientations were generally close to the true average orientations but varied across participants.
[context]
Eleven adults completed all experiments. Experiment 1 used six identical Gabor patterns at one of six orientations from -75 degrees to 75 degrees in 30-degree steps. Experiment 2A used four stimulus sets of six differently oriented Gabor patterns with true average orientations of 45 degrees or -45 degrees, with and without elements matching the average orientation. Experiment 2B used continuous bar adjustment to report perceived average orientation. EEG was recorded at 1000 Hz from 32 electrodes with a reference electrode located between Fz and Cz, and impedance was kept below 5 kOhm. Signals were downsampled to 500 Hz, band-pass filtered between 1 and 80 Hz, epoched from -1000 ms to +1500 ms around stimulus onset, baseline corrected from -300 ms to 0 ms, and cleaned by removing artifact components identified by automatic labeling when the estimated artifact probability exceeded 50 percent. Mean retained trials were 922 plus or minus 136 in Experiment 1 and 444 plus or minus 150 in Experiment 2A. The proposed method differs by planning to re-analyze a previously published EEG dataset in which participants monitored for targets, thereby avoiding motor responses during critical analysis periods, whereas the paper’s data were newly collected and included button-press or bar-adjustment responses.
[end related work 2]

