- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 639
[method]
The paper describes a time-locked M/EEG searchlight method for locating multivariate neural effects in space and time. It loads time-locked MEG epochs, treats each trial as an independent chunk, reassigns samples into two halves for a split-half correlation measure, and applies a searchlight using a channel-by-time neighborhood. The neighborhood is formed by crossing a spatial neighborhood of 10 sensor locations with a temporal neighborhood spanning 5 consecutive time bins. The measure computes split-half correlations and produces maps over channels, over time, and in channel-by-time space. This is related to the proposed EEG analysis because it uses time-locked M/EEG data and multivariate searchlight inference to characterize neural representations over time, although it uses split-half correlation rather than linear discriminant analysis or inverted encoding modeling. The paper also describes threshold-free cluster enhancement as a cluster-based multiple comparison correction approach, which is related to the proposed use of cluster-corrected statistical analyses for EEG time courses.
[results]
No quantitative classification accuracy, correlation magnitudes, or corrected statistical thresholds are reported for the M/EEG example. The reported outcome is that the searchlight maps identify when and where 20 Hz median nerve stimulation is detectable in the MEG data, with separate maps localizing effects across channels, across time, and in channel-by-time space. No baseline comparison, effect size, or significance value is provided.
[context]
The M/EEG example used Neuromag306 gradiometer data from 145 trials, with 145 epochs during stimulation and 145 pre-stimulation epochs from the same trials. Each trial was treated as an independent chunk, and samples were then split into two chunks for split-half correlation. The channel neighborhood used 10 sensor locations and combined planar gradiometers into one value per sensor location; the time neighborhood used a radius of 2, yielding 5 time bins. The significance-testing section recommends threshold-free cluster enhancement over fixed-threshold cluster correction for whole-brain or sensor-time maps, which is relevant to the proposed cluster-corrected EEG time-course analysis.
[end related work 1]

